WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:06.930
STEPHAN DE BARSE: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:10.160
This is Stephan De
Barse with o9 Solutions.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.150
At o9, we are digitally
transforming how businesses

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:16.690
plan and operate today.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:18.550
I'm based out of Amsterdam.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:20.650
I'm running o9 in
Europe, as well as

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:23.000
our global
go-to-market practice.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.590
Today, we're going to talk about
AI-powered integrated business

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:28.720
spending on Google Cloud.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.620
What is the agenda for today?

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:34.900
We'll start with a quick
introduction into o9 Solutions,

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:38.900
followed by the relevance of
planning in today's world,

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:43.400
where we'll share a couple of
learnings from the pandemic.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.430
Then we will talk
about the architecture

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:48.520
of what we call the Future
Ready Enterprise, followed

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:53.240
by a couple of use cases in
retail and manufacturing.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.050
Then we'll close out
with the key contacts,

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:59.840
so that you can engage
with us going forward.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.790
What is o9 all about?

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:06.500
o9 is a premier AI platform for
integrated business planning

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.330
and digital operations.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:12.680
We support companies in getting
to a more accurate forecast,

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:16.090
a faster response, and
end-to-end visibility.

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:20.000
The o9 platform is designed
for Fortune 500 companies.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.240
We're working with leaders
in every single industry.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.330
In retail, think about
Walmart and Starbucks,

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:29.720
where we help with forecasting
replenishment and integrated

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.000
flow planning.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.720
In consumer products companies
like Nestlé and AB InBev,

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.230
as well as Estée Lauder.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:39.320
We support those companies
in demand forecasting,

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:43.010
supply planning, and
integrated business planning.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:45.500
Over the last few
years, the o9 platform

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.960
has been adopted by
many other leaders

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:51.410
in different industries, like
Exxon Mobil in oil and gas,

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:54.080
and T-Mobile in telecom.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.220
Last but not least,
Google is an o9 client.

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.860
We are supporting
Google consumer hardware

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:03.050
in better predicting
demand, as well

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:06.750
as doing the supply planning and
integrated business planning.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:10.729
Why are so many of those Fortune
500 companies selecting o9?

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:13.130
That is because we have
developed this next-generation

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:15.560
planning platform
that is able to cope

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.410
with the complexity
of organizations,

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:23.890
as well as using that technology
to help them better planning.

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:27.880
Then we have designed a UI and
UX that, from an enterprise

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.040
point of view, feels
like your Google

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:33.280
device or your
Google applications,

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:38.920
and hence, it is architected
to drive user adoption.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:42.370
Lastly, time to value
is extremely important.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:46.440
Our implementations are
short or fast and full

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:51.710
of very agile
implementation methodology.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.020
What I want to do
with you next is share

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:57.410
a couple learnings
from this pandemic.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:02.090
What have large retailers and
large manufacturers experienced

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.550
throughout the past
couple of months?

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:07.250
First, demand variability.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:10.190
It's all about,
can I detect better

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:13.140
the risks and opportunities
on the demand side?

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:16.550
What we have seen is those
products mix shifts, right?

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:18.530
An increase in
essential products,

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:20.690
but a decrease in others.

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:22.040
Channel shifts, right?

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:26.780
Overnight, we went from the
traditional brick and mortar

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:28.340
to online.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.970
We've also seen regional
demand variability,

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:34.940
with lockdown and reopening
schedules varying by country,

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:38.060
by city, in some
cases by zip code.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:41.090
So the question is,
many organizations,

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:45.050
how do you now predict
demand when all of this

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:47.840
happens in a very
short amount of time?

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.210
That is a challenge
for many, and that

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:53.780
is something we support
global companies with.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:56.810
Second is on the supply side.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:59.090
Again, it's all about
early detection.

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:03.110
Many companies today do
not have the visibility

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:07.250
or the capacities, material
availability, and lead times

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:09.290
of their suppliers,
goal [? backers, ?]

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:11.300
or contract manufacturers.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.840
What we have observed is that
supplies and suppliers are

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.990
slowing down or
stopping production,

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:18.920
but some are coming back online.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:23.150
So how do I know on which
one I can rely, and when?

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:26.270
Right now, what we have seen
is that inventory and capacity

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:29.550
visibility across a
network is challenging.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:32.010
There are multiple
ERPs, multiple systems,

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:35.660
and they don't have that
visibility quickly enough.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:38.210
And then lastly, global
companies struggle

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:41.720
which visibility into
their supplier network.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:46.190
So very often, they do not
know what suppliers can commit,

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:50.370
and what their capacities and
material availabilities are.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:54.300
Third is integrated
planning and response.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:59.520
How fast is an organization
able to run scenarios?

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.480
Demand can be 90, demand can
be 100, demand can be 110.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:05.760
Well, what is my
supply chain response?

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:07.800
What is my true cost to serve?

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.590
And do I understand the
different assumptions

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:12.900
that go into those
different scenarios?

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:17.220
Companies today lack the ability
to run fast scenario planning,

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:20.250
understanding all the
different tradeoffs,

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:22.560
including the financial ones.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.220
Again, that is something
where we provide support.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:30.750
The other two key learnings is
that enterprise collaboration

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:32.540
goes digital.

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:35.750
What we have seen is
that whether companies

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:38.090
have production planners
or supply planners

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:43.040
or demand planners or
commercial planners, overnight,

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:46.330
all of those functions
had to work from home.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:49.060
And what we've seen
is that that boosted

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:51.730
online and digital
collaboration,

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:54.460
but also showed the
possibilities of looking

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:59.920
into more centralized
and more specialized COEs

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:02.440
that companies can deploy.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:05.770
Lastly, what we've seen is
digital operating models

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:08.190
for structural transformation.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:11.280
CXOs that want
structural improvement

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:14.140
involve resilience and cost.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:16.890
That means that this
whole collaboration

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:20.610
doesn't stop within the four
walls of the enterprise.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:23.490
The question is, how
can we go beyond that

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:26.300
and start connecting with
suppliers and customers

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:30.880
in more real time, use that
knowledge to then shape demand,

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:33.790
and better collaborate
with suppliers and contract

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:35.720
manufacturers.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:38.810
So we have observed a couple
of key trends, a couple

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:41.270
of key learnings, and
we have definitely

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:44.480
seen the need for
global companies

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:48.740
to digitally transform how
they do and execute integrated

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:51.580
business planning today.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:56.170
Now, what I want to do
next is introduce you

00:06:56.170 --> 00:06:58.390
to the o9 platform,
and I would like

00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:01.930
to do that by using an
example from Google Maps.

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:06.200
In this example, Google is able
to tell us, hey, leave by 8:45

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:09.430
to arrive at the airport
one and a half hours

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:11.470
before your flight.

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:14.480
Now, we all take this kind
of technology for granted,

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:16.770
but to me, it's a
beautiful example

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:23.040
of how we have moved from sort
of traditional data and data

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:26.350
mining to prescriptive
decision making.

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:29.010
Now, let's take this
example to the enterprise.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:32.160
Assume that we could sense
that demand is going up.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.370
What do I instantly
want to understand?

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:37.260
Can I satisfy that
demand, yes or no?

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:39.720
If not, then I want
to understand why not.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:41.240
Where are my constraints?

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:44.645
Is it a factory constraint,
a material constraint,

00:07:44.645 --> 00:07:47.130
a logistics constraint?

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:49.710
But it doesn't
need to stop there.

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:52.950
What I truly want is a
prescriptive recommendation,

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:55.480
like Google Maps is giving
us here, and saying,

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:58.500
hey, I found out that
we have this logistics

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:00.660
constraint, but no worries--

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:02.850
we can set up an
expedited lane so you

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:04.830
can fulfill the demand on time.

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:06.890
Oh, and by the way,
the additional cost

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:10.710
of that expedite are X.
And that is the notion

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:12.900
of digital transformation
in the planning

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:15.820
space in the enterprise.

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:18.570
Now, what is actually
behind this example

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:20.370
that Google Maps is using?

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:23.550
Three core capabilities that
I will link to the enterprise

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:25.950
and to the o9
platform in a second.

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:27.630
In the middle,
you find something

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:30.640
that we call the
AI-powered knowledge model.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:34.179
So in the Google Maps example,
there is network master data.

00:08:34.179 --> 00:08:36.059
Think about the
map with the roads

00:08:36.059 --> 00:08:38.340
and the traffic lights
and the speed limits.

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:40.980
That is obviously connected
with the big data store.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:43.200
What do we find in
the big data store?

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.390
Location tracking,
calendar information,

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:46.860
and the flight status.

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:50.610
All those data elements
help us spotting risks

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:52.850
and opportunities much earlier.

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:56.260
So now, when you combine network
master data with big data

00:08:56.260 --> 00:09:00.120
and you run smart algorithms,
now on your mobile device,

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:05.220
you can now receive prescriptive
actions with predictive alerts.

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:09.090
And again, that is where we
need to take enterprise decision

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:13.920
making, so that that becomes
as easy and as prescriptive

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.470
as this example
with Google Maps.

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:20.670
So at o9, we developed
the following platform

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:23.160
that allows us in
the transformation

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:25.410
to prescriptive decision making.

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:26.900
What you can see
in the middle is

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:28.400
what we call an
enterprise knowledge

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:32.190
graph, the digital
brain of the enterprise.

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:35.610
That is allowing us to create
a full digital representation

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:36.960
of the enterprise.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:39.870
From the supply chain, the
supplier nodes, the material

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:42.240
hubs, the factories
to the shippers,

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:44.190
connecting that with
the different demand

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:46.770
nodes, the products,
the initiatives,

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:50.625
the sales accounts-- all the way
to the go-to-market channels,

00:09:50.625 --> 00:09:52.500
whether it's selling
through retail, business

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:56.640
to business, or to
stores or online,

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:58.920
ultimately all the way
down to the end consumer,

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:02.710
where we can also bring in
data from the competitor.

00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:05.940
So that entire
knowledge brain is then

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:08.470
connected with
various data sensors.

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:09.990
What kind of data sensors?

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:12.630
Think about your ERP, where
we connect with master

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:14.800
data and transactional data.

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.010
But more importantly,
we want to connect

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:21.060
with data that gives us insights
in risks and opportunities,

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:23.610
so we can connect with
external data sources,

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:27.060
both structured and
unstructured from nature.

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:29.070
Can I give a couple of examples?

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:30.450
Absolutely.

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:34.020
Think about a company like
AB InBev, the largest beer

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:35.610
company in the world.

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:37.680
They're selling
through retail, they're

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:40.090
selling online, and
through distribution.

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:41.830
What is driving their demand?

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:42.870
Think about weather.

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:44.820
Think about competitive pricing.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:47.340
Think about competitive
promotions, their own trade

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:49.850
promotions, their
marketing initiatives.

00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:52.110
So what if we could
collect all the data

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:57.210
and use that more intelligently
to forecast the demand?

00:10:57.210 --> 00:10:59.240
Other example-- Starbucks.

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:01.040
What is driving their demand?

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:02.680
Think about local events--

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:07.760
a football game, or
maybe a graduation event,

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:11.870
when a Starbucks store is
located on a university campus.

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:14.630
Again, weather is
also a big influencer

00:11:14.630 --> 00:11:17.090
in terms of what's driving
demand at Starbucks.

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:19.730
So again, it's about
capturing that information

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:21.750
and bringing that in.

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:24.270
Is that only happening
with consumer products?

00:11:24.270 --> 00:11:25.260
Not really.

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:28.140
Think about companies like
Pirelli Tires or Bridgestone

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:29.230
Tires.

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:32.040
They're selling tires
to dealers and OEMs.

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:33.870
What is driving their demand?

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:36.660
That could be, you
know, the number of cars

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:38.850
being produced by the OEMs.

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:40.900
Can we incorporate
that kind of data?

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:42.730
And again, the answer is yes.

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:46.140
So the entire notion is, we are
looking out for all those data

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:50.010
sources that can either
predict a demand risk

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:53.190
or opportunity or a supply
risk and opportunity,

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:56.520
and integrate that
with the digital brain.

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:59.130
Then on top of the
digital brain is

00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:02.370
where we expose different
planning workflows.

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:05.490
On the far left hand side,
it's all about joint customer

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:09.150
collaboration, as well as better
connecting with my account

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:11.460
sales teams, category
management, and product

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:13.110
portfolio management.

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:15.730
Then when I jump into
the enterprise again,

00:12:15.730 --> 00:12:17.970
that is where my commercial
teams are connected

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:21.840
with my customers, and that is
where we need to support them

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:26.370
with annual operating planning,
initiative planning, demand

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:29.250
planning, gap closure
planning, and all

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:31.800
that combined with
analytics, because planning

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:34.020
and analytics go hand in hand.

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:36.120
On the same platform,
that is where you

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:38.480
can do supply chain planning.

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:41.250
We are creating a full digital
twin of the supply chain,

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:43.830
providing end-to-end
visibility and allowing

00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:48.600
for smart and intelligent demand
supply matching, control tower

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:50.940
capabilities, and
scenario planning.

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:53.130
Obviously we want
to connect that

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:55.780
with suppliers and
contract manufacturers,

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:57.780
as well as with all your
factories and logistics

00:12:57.780 --> 00:12:59.700
operators.

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:03.470
Then, similar to the commercial
side, planning and analytics

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:06.730
go hand in hand, which means
I can run analytics on my cost

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:09.500
to serve, inventory,
different planning policies,

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:11.260
and so forth.

00:13:11.260 --> 00:13:14.000
So all of this is
then coming together

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:16.470
in the sales and
operations planning.

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:18.450
No more meetings and PowerPoint.

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:22.350
It's fact-based decisions
live in the platform

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:24.990
where, again, the
system will prescribe

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:27.000
what actions to take
based on running

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:29.100
the different scenarios.

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:33.470
Now, all of this is obviously
across time horizons,

00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:36.060
so from the daily planning--

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:38.000
you know, the next
four weeks out--

